hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in his work on  fate where Cicero is considering a  fundamental metaphysical question about  whether the human being has any degree  of freedom or whether everything is  determined by some sort of necessity or  fate one of the key thinkers that he's  going to examine at considerable length  is cruciferous and cruciferous was the  third skull arc of the stoic school very  important school at the time that Cicero  was writing one that's had a lot of  influence down through history and the  Stoics had a very interesting  perspective on this question of freedom  and necessity unlike some others who  would be willing to say that everything  is totally determined there's nothing  you can do nothing is up to you you have  no choices so don't worry about any of  that would be the determinism or  fatalism side and unlike others who said  oh no there's complete human freedom you  can escape the bounds of necessity there  may be some things that are necessitated  but nothing that really applies to human  minds or choices or actions the stoic  said it's actually both  and so this is what in the discussion of  free will and determinism we call  compatibilism and Cicero in what he's  giving us of cruciferous here is  articulating one of the many  compatibilist positions that we find  throughout the history of ideas and so  when you're a compatible list you're  actually in some respect trying to  proverbial have-your-cake-  and-eat-it-too  which by the way you can do you can  actually have a cake and eat it right  there's no problem with that in most  circumstances but they're trying to have  a determinism where everything is fated  or as Cicero is going to have  precipitous talk about following the  conventions of his time everything  happens of necessity or fate in some  respect every proposition about what  occurs is either true or false including  ones about the future he wants to have  that and at the same time he wants to  have some degree of freedom freedom of  what freedom of the body no freedom of  the human mind and Cicero actually tells  us that he regards cruciferous as being  in this kind of middle position he says  that cruciferous stood is an unofficial  umpire and wished to strike a compromise  as a matter of fact he inclines to  adhere to those who held that the mind  is released from all necessity of motion  that is the mind can determine itself  there are some things that are  volitional that are our own choice but  Cicero also points out because of the  formulas because of the language that he  used that's peculiar to himself he slips  into difficulties that against his will  he lends support to the necessity of  fate so let's take a look now at what  Cicero provides us of cruciferous  doctrines here we should mention too  that we don't actually possess any  writings by cruciferous as such but  there are fragments and portions of his  works not only in Cicero but in several  other philosophers as well so as a stoic  as somebody who believes that everything  is material and that there is a kind of  ruling logos running through things  cruciferous argues that all motion all  change everything that is the way it is  and everything that's going to be  different is caused is caused by  precedent or antecedent causes that is  things that happen before it lead to  things coming after it and if you put  all these together in some sort of  causal Nexus then you can say that  everything is happening by fate and this  is something that cruciferous is  committed to and the Stoics in general  are committed to he goes a little bit  further in saying that everything arises  and here he's using the word eternal in  a somewhat different manner perhaps then  later philosophers will do meaning from  eternity causes that have always been  and and you know in some respect will  always have an influence so internal  causes governing future events  everything unrolls  in this succession where cause leads to  effect that effect leads to other causes  and so on and so on and so on  so we have a deterministic universe that  we belong to a determined universe  seemingly in every single respect now  how do we get any freedom out of this  this is where we have to make some  important distinctions and that is  precisely one of the things that  cruciferous did that is a contribution  to this idea or debate or understanding  and that's part of what Cicero is  highlighting here so there are some  distinctions and some clarifications  about the notion of cause in the  strictest sense we should only use the  word  for what makes the effect come about  right what produces the effect and  Cicero says that it's not just enough to  proceed to be before the effect it has  to precede it effectively the word there  is a faqih inter coming from the word to  create or to bring about or to produce  saucony right so the cause has to in a  certain respect produce the effect it's  not enough that it contributes to it  it's not enough that it's just they're  doing something in the background it has  to really be central to this a little  bit later Cicero is going to discuss  what we could call necessary conditions  and this is particularly important as  well as says they declare and you means  the Stoics that there is a difference  whether a thing the thing that we're  considering as a cause is of such a kind  that something cannot be effected  without it so what we're talking about  there is something being a necessary  condition for something else so if we  think about he's not using this as an  example but if we think about an example  of going out and fishing with worms and  a hook you know you you don't  necessarily have to have a rowboat but  you could be in a rowboat out in the  middle of the lake or you could cast  from the shore the hook is the cause in  a certain respect of the fish being  caught right but if you throw the hook  out by itself unless you have a really  stupid fish or very hungry right if  they're defeating frenzied it's not  gonna bite a bare hook by itself you  need to put some bait on the hook and  then you could come up with other things  we could create lures or you go on all  sorts of other examples with respect to  fishing but just imagine putting a worm  on the hook in the old-fashioned manner  of  of fishing right the worm is what the  fish actually sees gravitates towards  bites down on the hook then penetrates  them to its mouth and then you reel it  in or perhaps pull it in if there's no  real on your fishing rod which of those  is the cause well you do need the worm  but the worm is just a necessary  condition the worm doesn't cause in any  very strict sense the fish being caught  it's a matter of fact you could probably  point at each of the things and say you  know rod line hook worm not all those  are just necessary conditions none of  them are actually the single cause so he  says there's a difference between  whether a thing is of such a kind that  nothing cannot be affected without it or  such that nothing or that or such that  something must necessarily be affected  by it a cause necessitates the effect a  cause in the strict sense so perhaps the  hook you could say is the cause of the  fish being able to be reeled in in a  certain respect because it's hooked  through the flesh though the hook is the  cause of the damage to the fish right  and the worm is just a proximate cause  in that example so it's not just what's  necessary conditions for effects cícero  goes on and he says none of the causes I  mentioned is really a cause because none  by its own force affects the thing of  which it is said to be the cause nor is  that which is a condition other things  being affected a cause but that of which  the access necessarily produces the  thing of which it is the cause and going  back now to this notion of of cause and  the language that we're using  cruciferous is willing to allow us to  use the term provided we make an  important distinction  so some causes are perfect and  principal ruling right they actually do  bring about the effect others just make  some sort of contribution they're part  of the causal Nexus but they are not the  single thing that determines that he  calls those auxiliary that is helpful  you could say and proximate the  auxiliaries in the Roman army were the  people who weren't part of the legions  but fought on the side of it and you  know helped out they weren't to be  relied upon as the main principal thing  so we can distinguish between these  different kinds of causes where is the  upshot for this well we see this bearing  its fruit when we look at this famous  example of the cylinder or the top that  cruciferous used to try to explain the  compatibilist position about the human  mind and it's very important to remember  that this is an analogy this is not a  exact one-to-one correspondence he's not  saying we are a cylinder or we are a top  the human mind works differently but  there is a structural resemblance here  so what is the idea behind the cylinder  at the top you have a cylinder which is  round and it can roll down an incline or  you have a top which is a cone and you  spin it right you you or maybe you do it  with your fingers you set it spinning  which of those is the cause is pushing  the cylinder the cause of it rolling or  is it the nature of the cylinder itself  Chris tempest says that there's a very  important difference here you're just  adding a motion to it you are causing in  a certain sense but you're an auxilary  and proximate cause you're not the  principal cause for the cylinder rolling  the nature of the cylinder the fact that  it's round rather than square is why it  rolls and can  ten years rolling similarly with the top  you started spinning but then it  continues on its own motion which you  imparted to it but it does that because  of its nature you can't say spin a book  I mean you can try and maybe if you're  super lucky you find a surface on which  you could actually spin this but it's  not of that nature now the human mind  what about its desires its appetites and  its a sense the Stoics want to claim  that these are actually up to us or in  our power in in potus thought a is how  Cicero talks about it which is very  similar to the language that's used  later on by people like epictetus and  who mean up to us in our control so this  is a consistent doctrine the Stoics  wanted to say that desire to some degree  is within our power assent even more so  is yet desire can be caused by outside  things you see the fruit you're hungry  and you start to you know desire eating  that fruit you see a beautiful person of  whatever gender you're attracted to and  you feel sexual desire towards them now  desire is one thing and we feel it right  and when the Stoics also talk about a  version which is the sort of  corresponding thing away from desire you  know you see something gross maybe some  garbage and you're like I don't want to  touch that right or you get something  slimy on your hands and you want to go  and wash your hands  that's a desire a sin is something  further than that that is where you  yourself sort of take that on ratify it  make it your own and the Stoics thought  as many other philosophers do it's  possible to have a desire that comes to  you without you actually choosing it's  caused by something on the  although it's cause because of your  human nature as well and then you can  choose whether to assent to it or not  now if everything was fated by necessity  it would seem that you actually don't  have any choice about a scent  cruciferous says no you actually do have  choice about a scent but it is also  faded there are causes for it but you  yourself your human nature the mind that  you've created to some degree right  through your own working of yourself  through the choices you've made the  thoughts you've thought the willings  you've willed that is the cause for  whether you assent or not to this or  that desire so on the one hand you are  determined there is a quasi lexus but  you're part of that causal Nexus and  you've determined part of what that  causal Nexus looks like so you're also  in another respect free not greatly free  of course because you don't control  really anything outside of the  boundaries of your mind for the Stoics  but there is a certain sort of freedom  there and this is a compatibilist  position on the issue of freedom and  determinism some people may look at this  and say that really doesn't seem like  freedom to me so long as there is a  causal Nexus you're just talking about  us having a sort of extra role at the  end of all these other causes  but the Stoics and cruciferous really  did think that to some degree this does  at least as far as the mind goes lie  within our power so there is some  freedom there even though there is a  nexus of causes precisely because we  ourselves are one of those causes the  nature that we have and make for ourself  become in this case a perfect and  primary cause  you
 

